window.docContents[251].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Conservation-and-Incompressibility/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow--From-Transport-to-Fluid-Motion":{"contents":"The simplest evolution in the hierarchy is a scalar carried by a prescribed\nvelocity. The transport equation\n\n\n\nsays that  is constant along particle paths. The velocity is known,\nso the equation is linear in the transported scalar. The\ncharacteristic chain\nrule is the scalar counterpart of the\nparticle-path chain rule.\n\nIn one space dimension, allowing the transported quantity to determine its\nown speed gives inviscid Burgers,\n. Characteristics can intersect, smooth\nsolutions can form shocks, and weak solutions then require an entropy\ncondition. Incompressible Euler retains the self-transport mechanism, but the\nvelocity is vector-valued and pressure enforces the divergence-free\nconstraint.\n\nA fluid is described in Eulerian variables by fields attached to positions in\nspace: density , velocity , and pressure . A\nparticle released at  follows a trajectory  satisfying\n\n\n\nIf a scalar or vector field  is observed along that particle path, the\nchain rule gives\n\n\n\nThis is the material derivative. For prescribed  it is linear in the\ntransported field . The fluid nonlinearity appears when , because\nthe unknown velocity then transports itself.\n\nA particle path is represented by the local identityThe pointwise predicate is useful when only a local calculation is needed.\nIts setwise form requires the same identity throughout a time interval.\n\nIf  is a particle path, the associated spacetime curve\n has velocity . Therefore every\ndifferentiable spacetime field  satisfiesFor scalar , expanding the Fréchet derivative gives the checked identity\n\nWrite the spacetime trajectory as the sum of its time coordinate and the\nzero-time embedding of . Their derivatives are respectively the time\nbasis vector and the embedded spatial velocity. The ordinary Fréchet chain\nrule gives the directional derivative, and expansion in the Euclidean\ncoordinate basis separates its time coordinate from the spatial sum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Conservation and Incompressibility\u0009From Conservation Laws to Incompressible Flow","header":"8.2.1.1. From Transport to Fluid Motion","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Conservation-and-Incompressibility/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Conservation-and-Incompressibility--From-Conservation-Laws-to-Incompressible-Flow--From-Transport-to-Fluid-Motion"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Quantitative-Time-Sobolev-Embedding--Quantitative-time-embedding":{"contents":"Throughout these estimates, assume .\n\nFor a Gelfand inclusion , a trajectory\n, and a dual weak derivative\n, define\n\nThe two terms of this constant correspond to the two steps of the estimate.\nThe first dominates the interval average of , a value\nthat the squared norm of the continuous representative must attain at some\ntime; the second bounds, through the energy identity, how far the squared\npivot norm can move between that time and any other point of .\n\nOn a nontrivial interval , every Lions–Magenes representative satisfiesConsequently, uniform  and  bounds give a\nuniform pivot-space bound without an additional \nhypothesis.\n\nChoose a time where the squared pivot norm equals its interval average.\nThe inclusion operator bounds that average by\n. Apply the exact energy identity between\nthat time and , then use the duality estimate and  Hölder to bound\nthe pairing integral by .\n\nIn the identity triple , . The Riesz isometry identifies the\ndual weak derivative with a Hilbert-valued function ,\ngiving the familiar one-dimensional Sobolev bound for .\n\nFor Hilbert-valued data  in , the explicit\none-dimensional time-Sobolev bound is\n\nBecause the representative agrees with the Bochner trajectory off a null\nset, the bound also has an almost-everywhere form for the trajectory itself,\na statement that makes sense without choosing a representative.\n\nIf du is the weak time derivative of u on a nontrivial finite interval,\nthen the Lions–Magenes representative for the identity Gelfand triple is continuous\non the closed interval and satisfiesfor every . The original Bochner trajectory satisfies the same\nestimate almost everywhere.\n\nChoose an interior time at which the squared norm equals its interval\naverage.  The exact Hilbert-space energy identity controls the change from\nthat time to an arbitrary endpoint, and the  Hölder inequality bounds\nthe resulting dual pairing integral.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Quantitative Time-Sobolev Embedding","header":"6.3.1.9.1. Quantitative time embedding","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Quantitative-Time-Sobolev-Embedding--Quantitative-time-embedding"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Trace":{"contents":"Boundary values of a Sobolev function cannot be defined by evaluating an\narbitrary Lp representative. A trace theorem instead constructs a bounded\noperator by proving an estimate where classical evaluation makes sense and\nthen extending that map to the completed Sobolev space.\n\nFor example, take the constant function  on . Assigning\nthe value zero or seven at the endpoints changes neither its interior\n class nor its weak derivative. Its trace is nevertheless  at\nboth endpoints, as agreement with the smooth constant function requires.\nThe trace recovers boundary values from controlled interior approximation;\narbitrary values assigned on the boundary cannot change it.\n\nThe construction follows the geometry. Endpoint evaluation on the positive\nhalf-line supplies the one-dimensional estimate. Integrating the same\nestimate over tangential variables gives the flat half-space trace. Boundary\ncharts, a partition of unity, and the atlas's flattening operators then\nassemble a trace on an operational  domain; the global extension\noperator is not used. The kernel of the trace is , the closure of\nthe test functions, which is why membership in  is the formal\nreplacement for “vanishes on the boundary” in weak Dirichlet problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings","header":"4.3.2. Trace","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Trace"}});